course: dictionary
lesson: 9417
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around it, which are its context. «The fireworks show was a damp squib in the rain.» A real firework cannot be damp and still go off, and the show is about people being entertained, so squib here means a disappointment.
explain: A squib is first a small firework that hisses and then bangs. Squibs and fireworks are dangerous for children to handle, so always ask an adult. Read the sentence again with your meaning in place of the word, and check it still makes sense.
fact: A squib is a small firework that hisses and then bangs.
fact: «A damp squib» (UK) means something expected to be exciting that turns out to be a disappointment.
fact: Squib can also mean a short, funny or sharp piece of writing that mocks someone (old use).
fact: In Australian slang, «Don't be a squib» means don't be scared.
fact: Squibs and fireworks are dangerous for children to handle.
exercise: «The fireworks show was a damp squib in the rain.» What does a damp squib mean here? | A loud bang that scared the dogs | A short, funny poem about the rain | A huge, exciting success | *A disappointment that did not live up to its promise
exercise: What is a squib in its first meaning? | A big bonfire on the beach | A kind of sweet from the shop | *A small firework that hisses and then bangs | A wet towel left out in the rain
exercise: In Australian slang, «Don't be a squib» means what? | Don't forget your coat | Don't eat too many sweets | *Don't be scared | Don't be late
test: What does squib mean in «The show was a damp squib»? | A friend | A loud bang | A firework | *A disappointment
test: Which clue in «The fireworks show was a damp squib in the rain» helps you know it is not about a real firework? | The word in | The word fireworks | *The show and the rain, which spoil the fun | The word squib
exam: Which is NOT a meaning of squib in the dictionary? | A small firework that hisses and then bangs | *A large tree that grows in gardens | A coward in Australian slang | A disappointing let-down
